    "rationale": "Evidence across five races: the iteration-0 baseline (sensors at x = ±0.024, ±0.012, 0 and y = 0.05 m, 12 mm footprint, 0.6 kg, 0.12 m track, 0.03 m radius) is the only design that finishes any track. It finishes the oval 20/20 at about 1.65 s, and goes off track on the s-bend (progress 0.17) and slalom (progress 0.12). Moving the sensors forward to y = 0.07 m (iteration 3) collapsed the oval to 0/20. Moving them back to y = 0.03 m (iteration 4) also collapsed the oval to 0/20, while raising s-bend progress to 0.30 and oval progress to 0.48. Lead therefore has a sharp optimum at y = 0.05 m for the oval, and it trades oval stability against bend progress. The wider array with a 0.5 kg mass (iteration 2) also lost oval finishes (15/20), and the 0.10 m track with the 0.033 m radius (iteration 1) lost more (17/20). Spread, track and mass changes have not helped the bends. The one lever not yet tested is sensor footprint. I therefore restore the baseline geometry exactly, including y = 0.05 m, and change only the footprint from 12 mm to 8 mm. The 12 mm pitch is kept, so the outer sensors still span ±0.024 m, and the 25 mm tape is still always covered by at least one sensor. Expected result: a finer edge resolution should give the controller a sharper estimate of the line position on the bends, so the s-bend and slalom progress should rise above 0.17 and 0.12. The risk is that the smaller footprint reads more noise or switches more abruptly, which could hurt the oval finish. The oval is the reference for keeping the baseline, so a drop there would argue against footprint as well.",
